--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertNull`, `assertSame` against `null` in either order and `assertEquals` against `null` are one predicate over every kind of value a program can build -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`, `rule:classes/comparable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `test-assert-null-refuses-every-value-that-is-not-null.nvst` asks two of
// these spellings over eighteen *scalars* and the empty array. The kinds it
// cannot reach with a literal are the ones where the three members might
// plausibly part: a value behind a reference, a `Core` instance, a callable,
// binary data, a container of containers, and a shape. All of those are one
// `Tag::Object`-or-container answer to `assertNull`'s `matches!` and one call
// into `identity::value_identical` for the other two, so the claim is that
// none of them is a special case in either.
//
// `assertEquals` is the member with the most room to disagree here: `rule:classes/comparable`
// sends *two objects* to `Comparable::compareTo`, and an object beside
// `null` is not two objects -- so it must fall back to the identical answer
// rather than ask the class anything. The object row is what makes that a
// claim rather than a restatement.
//
// The symmetric spelling is `assertSame($nothing, $subject)` with `$nothing` a
// `mixed` binding, not the literal `null` first. `assertSame`'s two parameters
// are one `T` bound to argument 1, so `assertSame(null, $subject)` binds `T`
// to `null` and is `E0401: expected 'null', found 'mixed'` at the second
// argument -- the member's argument order is fixed by its signature, and a
// `mixed` binding is the only way to ask identity's other direction at all.

enum Suit {
    Clubs,
}

final class Card {
    public int $rank = 3;
}

final class Ask {
    public static function nullish(mixed $subject): bool {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertNull($subject);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromNull) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static function sameAsNull(mixed $subject): bool {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertSame($subject, null);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromSame) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static function nullIsSame(mixed $subject): bool {
        try {
            mixed $nothing = null;
            Core\Test::assertSame($nothing, $subject);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromReversed) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static function equalsNull(mixed $subject): bool {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertEquals($subject, null);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromEquals) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    // The control: identity is reflexive, so every row below holds here. It is
    // what stops the counts from being satisfied by a member that refuses
    // everything it is handed.
    public static function reflexive(mixed $subject): bool {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertSame($subject, $subject);
            return true;
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromReflexive) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

Card $card = new Card();
callable $callable = fn (): int => { return 1; };
bytes $binary = "ab" as bytes;
array<array<int>> $nested = [[1], [2]];
object $shape = {a: 1};
Core\Time\Duration $duration = 30s;
?Card $present = $card;
?Card $missing = null;
array<string> $empty = [];
?string $noFirst = Core\Arr::first($empty);

// Eight kinds, none of them `null`: a user object, a callable, `bytes`, a
// container of containers, an anonymous shape, a `Core` instance built from
// a `rule:types/duration` duration like `1h`, an enum case, and a `?T` binding that is
// holding one. Every one must be refused by all four spellings.
array<mixed> $values = [
    $card, $callable, $binary, $nested, $shape, $duration, Suit::Clubs, $present,
];

int $agreed = 0;
int $held = 0;
int $reflexive = 0;
foreach ($values as tainted string $kind => mixed $value) {
    bool $isNull = Ask::nullish($value);
    bool $same = Ask::sameAsNull($value);
    bool $reversed = Ask::nullIsSame($value);
    bool $equal = Ask::equalsNull($value);
    if ($isNull == $same && $same == $reversed && $reversed == $equal) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    if ($isNull) {
        $held = $held + 1;
    }
    if (Ask::reflexive($value)) {
        $reflexive = $reflexive + 1;
    }
}
echo "non-null: agreed=", $agreed, "/8 held=", $held, "/8 reflexive=", $reflexive, "/8\n";

// The same four spellings over the three ways a `null` arrives: the literal, a
// `?T` binding initialised to it, and a `?T` no literal wrote -- `Core\Arr::first`
// over an empty array answers one from inside a member. All four hold on all
// three, so the agreement above is not the four of them refusing in unison.
array<mixed> $nulls = [null, $missing, $noFirst];

int $nullAgreed = 0;
int $nullHeld = 0;
foreach ($nulls as tainted string $spelling => mixed $nothing) {
    bool $isNull2 = Ask::nullish($nothing);
    bool $same2 = Ask::sameAsNull($nothing);
    bool $reversed2 = Ask::nullIsSame($nothing);
    bool $equal2 = Ask::equalsNull($nothing);
    if ($isNull2 == $same2 && $same2 == $reversed2 && $reversed2 == $equal2) {
        $nullAgreed = $nullAgreed + 1;
    }
    if ($isNull2) {
        $nullHeld = $nullHeld + 1;
    }
}
echo "null: agreed=", $nullAgreed, "/3 held=", $nullHeld, "/3\n";
--EXPECT--
non-null: agreed=8/8 held=0/8 reflexive=8/8
null: agreed=3/3 held=3/3
